  "question": "Prove that for $x \\in (0,1)$, $\\sin^{-1}x + \\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{1-x^2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "Let $\\theta = \\sin^{-1}x$.<br /><br />Then $x=\\sin\\theta$, where $\\theta\\in(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$.<br /><br />Thus $\\sqrt{1-x^2}=\\sqrt{1-\\sin^2\\theta}=\\cos\\theta$.<br /><br />Hence $\\sin^{-1}(\\sqrt{1-x^2})=\\sin^{-1}(\\cos\\theta)$.<br /><br />Since $\\cos\\theta=\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta)$, we get $\\sin^{-1}(\\cos\\theta)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta$.<br /><br />Therefore $\\sin^{-1}x+\\sin^{-1}\\sqrt{1-x^2}=\\theta+(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
